      "content": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cassert>\n#include <climits>\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n\n    // Read matrix into flat array (row-major)\n    std::vector<int> mat(n * n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i * n + j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    assert(s > 0 && s < n && n > 0);\n\n    // Prefix sum matrix of size (n+1)x(n+1)\n    int ps_rows = n + 1;\n    std::vector<long long> ps(ps_rows * ps_rows, 0LL);\n\n    // Compute prefix sums\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int base_i = i * ps_rows;\n        int next_i = (i + 1) * ps_rows;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            long long val = mat[i * n + j];\n            ps[next_i + j + 1] = val + ps[base_i + j + 1] + ps[next_i + j] - ps[base_i + j];\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Find maximum sum of SxS submatrices\n    long long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n        int base_i = i * ps_rows;\n        int next_i = (i + s) * ps_rows;\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n            long long sum = ps[next_i + j + s] - ps[base_i + j + s]\n                          - ps[next_i + j] + ps[base_i + j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n",
      "path": "src/solution.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Replace the sliding-window algorithm with an integral-image (prefix-sum) approach that computes each SxS submatrix sum in O(1) time. This eliminates loop-carried dependencies present in the original sliding window updates, enabling full vectorization of both prefix computation and max-finding loops. Use OpenMP parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) to distribute independent submatrix evaluations across threads on ARM64.",
  "notes": "Memory usage increases due to storing (n+1)^2 long longs (~512MB at n=8000), but this is within typical HPC node limits. The algorithm remains correct and deterministic.",
